L["ascension"] = """After his suffering, Jesus presented himself to his disciples and gave many convincing proofs that he was alive. He appeared to them over a period of forty days and spoke about the kingdom of God. He ate with them, let them touch him, and opened their minds to understand the Scriptures. Those weeks changed frightened, scattered men into witnesses.

On one occasion, while he was eating with them, he gave them this command. Do not leave Jerusalem, but wait for the gift my Father promised, which you have heard me speak about. For John baptized with water, but in a few days you will be baptized with the Holy Spirit. So when they met together, they asked him, Lord, are you at this time going to restore the kingdom to Israel? They were still thinking about politics. Jesus answered, it is not for you to know the times or dates the Father has set by his own authority. But you will receive power when the Holy Spirit comes on you, and you will be my witnesses in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth.

After he said this, he was taken up before their very eyes, and a cloud hid him from their sight. They were looking intently up into the sky as he was going, when suddenly two men dressed in white stood beside them. Men of Galilee, they said, why do you stand here looking into the sky? This same Jesus, who has been taken from you into heaven, will come back in the same way you have seen him go into heaven.

Luke ends his gospel with the same event. While he was blessing them, he left them and was taken up into heaven. Then they worshiped him and returned to Jerusalem with great joy. And they stayed continually at the temple, praising God. There was joy, not sadness. They knew he was alive and reigning.

Why does this matter? The Ascension means that Jesus is now seated at the right hand of the Father. He reigns over everything. It also means that a human being is now in heaven, which is a promise for all who belong to him. And it was needed so that the Holy Spirit could come. Jesus had told them that it was for their good that he was going away.

What happened after? Ten days later, on the day of Pentecost, the Holy Spirit came on the believers with wind and fire. Peter preached, and three thousand people were added to the church that day. The disciples began to carry the message from Jerusalem outward, just as Jesus had said.

What does this mean for us today? Jesus' last words were not about heaven. They were about the work. We are called to be witnesses where we live, in our own Jerusalem, and as far as we can reach. We are not left alone, since he gave us the Spirit. And the angels' words are a promise. He will return. We live between the Ascension and his return, trusting, working and waiting.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus is the risen and ruling Lord, and that he sends his followers to carry his message to the whole world."""

L["stephen"] = """In the early church in Jerusalem, a man named Stephen stood out. The apostles chose him as one of seven men to oversee the care of widows who were being overlooked in the daily distribution of food. He was full of faith and of the Holy Spirit. Acts says that he was full of God's grace and power, and he performed great wonders and signs among the people.

Some men from a synagogue began to argue with Stephen, but they could not stand up against the wisdom the Spirit gave him as he spoke. So they secretly persuaded some men to say they had heard Stephen speak words of blasphemy. They stirred up the people, the elders and the teachers of the law. They seized Stephen and brought him before the Sanhedrin. At that, all who were sitting in the Sanhedrin looked intently at Stephen, and they saw that his face was like the face of an angel.

The high priest asked him if the charges were true. Stephen gave a long speech, going through Israel's history from Abraham, to Joseph, to Moses, to the temple. His point was that the people had always resisted God's messengers. You stiff-necked people! he said. You are just like your ancestors. Was there ever a prophet your ancestors did not persecute? They even killed those who predicted the coming of the Righteous One. And now you have betrayed and murdered him.

When the members of the Sanhedrin heard this, they were furious. But Stephen, full of the Holy Spirit, looked up to heaven and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing at the right hand of God. Look, he said, I see heaven open and the Son of Man standing at the right hand of God. Usually Jesus is described as seated at God's right hand. Here he stands, as if rising to welcome his servant.

At this they covered their ears and, yelling at the top of their voices, they all rushed at him. They dragged him out of the city and began to stone him. Meanwhile, the witnesses laid their coats at the feet of a young man named Saul. While they were stoning him, Stephen prayed, Lord Jesus, receive my spirit. Then he fell on his knees and cried out, Lord, do not hold this sin against them. When he had said this, he fell asleep.

Why does this matter? Stephen was the first Christian martyr. His death echoed the death of Jesus. He prayed for his killers as Jesus had, and he committed his spirit to the Lord. And the word the Bible uses for his death is that he fell asleep. For believers, death is not the end.

What happened after? A great persecution broke out against the church in Jerusalem, and the believers scattered throughout Judea and Samaria. But as they went, they preached the gospel wherever they were. The young man named Saul, who held the coats, was there. He would soon be knocked to the ground on the road to Damascus, meet the risen Jesus, and become the apostle Paul. Some have wondered whether Stephen's prayer was part of the reason.

What does this mean for us today? Few of us will be asked to give our lives, but we may be asked to speak up for the truth, and Stephen's calm and courage give us a model. He was not bitter. He forgave. And his story shows that God can bring good out of the worst events. The church was scattered, and the gospel spread further.

That is why this story is so important. It says that faithfulness may cost everything, and that the Lord is near to those who suffer for him. And it shows the power of forgiveness in the face of hatred."""
